<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Köse Dağ</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1243">1243</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-anatolia">Mongol invasions of Anatolia</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Köse Dağ was fought in 1243 in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>. The Mongols won decisively, reducing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. The resulting fragmentation of Anatolia into small principalities created the conditions from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> emerged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kose-dag.jpg" alt="Battle of Köse Dağ" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown. Catalonia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_K%C3%B6z%C3%A4_Dagh_(1243).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, which controlled large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> over the next 150 years. By the 1230s it had gained maritime and commercial strength through the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> and Sinope.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> reached Rum's frontier in the early 1230s by conquering western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Under their general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chormaqan">Chormaqan</a> they subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> and made the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Relations with Rum worsened after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaykhusraw-ii">Kaykhusraw II</a> became sultan in 1237, and Mongol raids began in 1240. In 1242 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a>, who had replaced the disabled Chormaqan, captured and pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a>, which opened a war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Kaykhusraw hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>, the Greek remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the Arab tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The Khwarazmian troops and the Turkomans who had joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babai-revolt">Babai revolt</a> refused to fight for him, and several capable commanders had been eliminated by his adviser Sa'd al-Din Köpek. The Mongol core numbered about 30,000, with Georgian and Armenian auxiliaries. Chroniclers put Kaykhusraw's army at 160,000 or 200,000, but the historian Bayarsaikhan Dashdondog estimates 80,000.</p><p>The army gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>. Experienced nobles urged a defensive stand, but younger nobles accused them of cowardice, and on 26 June the army attacked at the pass of Köse Dağ, about 80 kilometres north-west of Sivas. Baiju assembled a vanguard of the best soldiers of every unit and met the army of Rum in a narrow ravine.</p><p>For most of the day the army of Rum seemed slightly superior, but its formation collapsed in the evening. Sources differ on why: the Armenian historian Grigor of Akanc credited a Georgian prince, Aghbagha, while Josef Matuz favoured accounts of a feigned Mongol retreat followed by arrow fire and a charge. The army fled that night. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> suspected a trap in the deserted camp and lost the chance to capture the sultan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>. The vizier Muhezzibeddin negotiated surrender terms: a yearly tribute of 12 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, 500 bolts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and 5,000 sheep, and a Mongol overseer, or daruyachi. The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> submitted, and Cilician Armenia did so in 1244. After Kaykhusraw died in 1246 the realm split among factions of his three young sons. Seljuk power declined until it ended in 1308, and its lands passed to the Mongol Ilkhanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Köse Dağ” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seljuk authority had collapsed after the Mongol victory at the <b>Battle of Köse Dağ</b> in 1243, and Asia Minor had broken up into competing Turkic beyliks, petty principalities established along the shrinking Byzantine frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The army’s collapse came at the <b>Battle of Köse Dağ</b> in 1243, when the Mongols under Baiju routed Kaykhusraw II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li></ul></section>
